The first-appearance timeline gives a second view of the same phenomenon.

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

## From changing bases to factorials

A mixed-radix numeral uses the running products of its local bases as place weights. Extract digits by successive Euclidean division and evaluate them by accumulating those products.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

For radices 1,2,3,…, the weights are 0!,1!,2!,…. Explore the resulting canonical representation.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

## What to notice

In the Fibonacci story, stripping inherited factors isolates genuinely new arithmetic information. In the numeral story, stripping away fixed-base conventions reveals a universal running-product architecture. Both pipelines turn a structural theorem into a concrete algorithm with inspectable intermediate states.

